{"builder":"Goal: add a `--tsv` flag to the CLI so it reads tab-separated input. Make one pull request.\n\nCurrent state:\n- bin/csv2json.js reads all of stdin, calls `parse(text)` from src/parse.js, and writes `JSON.stringify(result)` plus a newline. It ignores process.argv.\n- src/parse.js has `export function parse(text, sep = ',')`. It splits rows on /\\r?\\n/ and splits fields with `l.split(sep)`.\n- test/csv.test.js uses node:test and has a `cli` test that runs `execFileSync('node', ['bin/csv2json.js'], { input, encoding: 'utf8' })`.\n- package.json has `\"test\": \"node --test\"`.\n- README.md shows only `node bin/csv2json.js < table.csv`.\n\nSteps:\n1. In bin/csv2json.js, check whether `process.argv.slice(2)` includes `'--tsv'`. The flag may appear anywhere among the arguments. If it is present, call `parse(text, '\\t')`. If not, call `parse(text)` exactly as the code does now. Leave the output format alone (`JSON.stringify(...)` + `\\n`). Do not change how unknown arguments are handled, and do not add --help, --sep or auto-detection. Do not change src/parse.js unless you have to; the existing `sep` parameter is enough.\n2. In test/ (for example test/csv.test.js), add node:test tests that run bin/csv2json.js through execFileSync with args `['bin/csv2json.js', '--tsv']` and tab-separated input, and assert the exact stdout:\n   - input `'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'` → `'[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'`\n   - input `'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n'` → `'[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n'`\n   - input `'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n'` → `'[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n'`\n   Also add a test without the flag: input `'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n'` → `JSON.stringify([{'a\\tb':'1\\t2'}]) + '\\n'`. Keep the existing tests.\n3. In README.md, add a section for the flag. It must include the example `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and say that `--tsv` reads tab-separated input (fields separated by a tab character). Keep the existing CSV example.\n4. Check the work from the repo root:\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv; echo \"exit=$?\"` → `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` then `exit=0`\n   - `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` → `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` → `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` → `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]`\n   - `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` → `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]`, where `\\t` is the literal two-character JSON escape\n   - `npm test` exits 0\n5. Commit the changes to bin/csv2json.js, test/ and README.md, and open the pull request. Its description should list the acceptance lines below.\n\nThe PR must meet these acceptance lines:\n- `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\n- With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\n- With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\n- Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\n- Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\n- `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\n- README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\n\nOut of scope: quoted fields or RFC 4180 rules, a general --sep/--delimiter option, separator auto-detection, --help, changes to how unknown arguments are handled, and changes to JSON formatting or how the header row is used.","verifier":"Check out the PR head. Run every command from the repo root and compare output byte for byte. A good way is to pipe it into `od -c`, or compare against a file made with printf. `npm install` is not needed; there are no dependencies.\n\nCheck 1. Expect: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline and exits 0.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/o1; echo \"exit=$?\"; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/o1 && echo SAME`.\nPASS: exit=0 and SAME. FAIL: any other exit code, or the output differs.\n\nCheck 2. Expect: With --tsv, commas inside a field stay literal: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'name\\tnote\\nx\\thello, world\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/o2; printf '[{\"name\":\"x\",\"note\":\"hello, world\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/o2 && echo SAME`.\nPASS: SAME. FAIL: the output differs.\n\nCheck 3. Expect: With --tsv and several data rows, every row comes out in order: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n3\\t4\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/o3; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"},{\"a\":\"3\",\"b\":\"4\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/o3 && echo SAME`.\nPASS: SAME. FAIL: the output differs.\n\nCheck 4. Expect: Without the flag, CSV still works as before: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]` followed by a newline.\nRun: `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/o4; printf '[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n' | cmp - /tmp/o4 && echo SAME`.\nPASS: SAME. FAIL: the output differs.\n\nCheck 5. Expect: Without the flag, tabs are not treated as separators: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` prints exactly `[{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}]` followed by a newline, which is the current output.\nRun: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/o5; printf '[{\"a\\\\\\\\tb\":\"1\\\\\\\\t2\"}]\\n' > /tmp/e5; cat /tmp/o5; cmp /tmp/e5 /tmp/o5 && echo SAME`. In the expected output, `\\t` is the literal two-character JSON escape (backslash then t), not a tab byte. Check /tmp/e5 with `od -c` and adjust the escaping if needed; or generate it with `node -e 'process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify([{\"a\\tb\":\"1\\t2\"}])+\"\\n\")' > /tmp/e5`.\nPASS: SAME, meaning one object whose key is `a<TAB>b` (JSON-escaped) and whose value is `1<TAB>2`. FAIL: the output differs, for example if the tabs were split into separate keys.\n\nCheck 6. Expect: `npm test` exits 0, and test/ contains at least one test that runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and checks its output.\nRun: `npm test; echo \"exit=$?\"`. Then run `grep -n -- '--tsv' test/*.js` and read the matching tests.\nPASS: exit=0, and at least one test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with the `--tsv` argument, gives it input that contains tab characters, and asserts on the output. FAIL: npm test exits non-zero, or no test in test/ runs bin/csv2json.js with --tsv on tab-separated input and asserts on its output.\n\nCheck 7. Expect: README.md documents the --tsv flag with an example invocation.\nRun: `grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md`, then read README.md.\nPASS: README.md mentions the --tsv flag, says it is for tab-separated input, and shows an example invocation such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv`. FAIL: the flag is not mentioned, or there is no example invocation.\n\nAdvisory (does not affect PASS/FAIL):\n- Check that the flag works in another position, e.g. `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js foo --tsv`.\n- Note any scope creep: --sep/--delimiter, auto-detection, --help, quoting rules, or changes to JSON formatting."}